---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="What is context?" description="What a context window holds in real terms, how it gets used up, and how big a window you need for the work you do." prose>
  <h1>What is context, and what does it mean for you?</h1>
  <p>
    Context is the model's desk. Everything it can see while it works has to be on that desk at once: your instructions, the stuff you
    handed it, its own thinking, and the answer it is writing. Whatever falls off the desk, it simply does not know about. No memory, no
    "I read that earlier". Gone.
  </p>
  <p>
    The size of the desk is measured in <b>tokens</b>. Forget the definition; what you need is the exchange rate. I measured it with real
    files from this repo, run through Qwen 3.5's tokenizer:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Stuff</th><th>Exchange rate</th><th>So 1,000 tokens is about</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Business English (reports, emails, transcripts)</td><td>about 1.5 tokens per word</td><td>670 words, a bit over one page</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Python code</td><td>about 19 tokens per line</td><td>50 lines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>JSON data</td><td>about 12 tokens per line</td><td>85 short lines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Markdown docs with links and tables</td><td>about 1.6 tokens per word</td><td>640 words</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note">Other models use other tokenizers, so expect 10 to 20 percent either way. Numbers, code, URLs and non-English text cost more per character than plain English.</p>

  <h2>The desk is never all yours</h2>
  <p>A context window is a budget, and four things spend it at the same time:</p>
  <div class="budget" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="b1">instructions</span><span class="b2">your material</span><span class="b3">thinking</span><span class="b4">answer</span>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Instructions:</b> the system prompt and your request. A few hundred tokens for a chat, 10,000 to 20,000 in agent tools before you type a word (every tool definition costs a few hundred).</li>
    <li><b>Your material:</b> the document, code, transcript or data.</li>
    <li><b>Thinking:</b> reasoning models write notes to themselves before answering. Easy questions take a few hundred tokens; hard puzzles can take 5,000 to 15,000.</li>
    <li><b>The answer:</b> a paragraph is 150 tokens, a page of report 800, a 100-line script about 2,000. In an agent, every tool result also lands on the desk and stays there.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <b>Rule of thumb used on this site: give your material at most half the window.</b> The other half is the room the model needs to
    actually do something with it. Fill the window to the brim and it can still read, but it cannot think.
  </p>

  <h2>What fits, window by window</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <label for="win" class="k">Pick a window size</label>
    <select id="win" style="max-width:220px">
      <option value="8">8k (8,192 tokens)</option>
      <option value="16" selected>16k (16,384 tokens)</option>
      <option value="32">32k (32,768 tokens)</option>
      <option value="64">64k (65,536 tokens)</option>
      <option value="128">128k (131,072 tokens)</option>
    </select>
    <div id="fit" style="margin-top:10px"></div>
  </div>
  <script>
    const T = {
      8: ['about 2,700 words, five pages', 'a 200-line script', 'a 15-minute meeting transcript',
        'answer questions about it, summarize it, fix one bug, or make three or four tool calls',
        'thinking hard: a reasoning model can burn the whole spare half on one tricky puzzle'],
      16: ['about 5,400 words, eleven pages', 'a 400-line module', 'a 30-minute meeting transcript',
        'summarize and categorize, write a function with its tests, or reason through a medium puzzle with thinking on',
        'agent work: tool definitions and results fill it after a handful of steps'],
      32: ['about 10,900 words, a 22-page report', 'around 850 lines of code, a handful of files', 'a one-hour meeting transcript',
        'analyze it and write a structured report, find patterns in a table, or run an agent loop of 10 to 15 tool calls',
        'comparing several long documents side by side'],
      64: ['about 21,800 words, a 45-page contract', 'around 1,700 lines, a small project', 'a two-hour workshop transcript',
        'compare documents, trace a bug across files, and surface insights across all of it',
        'a whole codebase, or a book'],
      128: ['about 43,700 words, a short novel such as Animal Farm with room to spare', 'around 3,400 lines, a real codebase slice', 'a four-hour workshop transcript',
        'synthesize many sources into one report, or run long agent sessions without forgetting the start',
        'speed on small hardware: on the 24 GB Mac, filling 64k already means minutes of waiting before the first word'],
    };
    const sel = document.getElementById('win');
    const out = document.getElementById('fit');
    const show = () => {
      const [words, code, talk, can, cant] = T[sel.value];
      out.innerHTML = `<p>Half the window for material holds <b>${words}</b>, or <b>${code}</b>, or <b>${talk}</b>.</p>
        <p>With the other half, it has room to <b>${can}</b>.</p><p class="note">Where it runs out: ${cant}.</p>`;
    };
    sel.addEventListener('input', show);
    show();
  </script>

  <h2>Advertised versus viable</h2>
  <p>
    A model card might say 256k. That is the size the model was built to accept, not the size you can use. Two things cut it down. First,
    memory: the model keeps notes on every token (the <a href="/learn/kv-cache/">KV cache</a>), and those notes have to fit beside the
    model in your GPU memory. Second, patience: reading a long prompt takes time before the first word appears. On the 24 GB Mac, Gemma
    took about seven minutes to answer a 64k prompt, nearly all of it spent reading.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is why this site measures <b>viable context</b>: the biggest window where the model still finds all three facts we hid in it,
    at a speed you would tolerate. Use the "Minimum viable context" filter on the <a href="/">compare page</a> with the table above: work
    out how big your material is, double it, and filter for that.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it yourself</h2>
  <p>Count the tokens in any file with a local model. Ollama reports how many tokens it read as <code>prompt_eval_count</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>{`# count_tokens.py  -  usage: python count_tokens.py myfile.txt
import json, sys, urllib.request

text = open(sys.argv[1], encoding="utf-8").read()
body = {"model": "qwen3.5:9b", "prompt": text, "raw": True, "stream": False,
        "options": {"num_predict": 1, "num_ctx": 131072}}
req = urllib.request.Request("http://localhost:11434/api/generate",
                             json.dumps(body).encode(), {"Content-Type": "application/json"})
n = json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(req))["prompt_eval_count"]
words = len(text.split())
print(f"{n} tokens, {words} words, {n / max(words, 1):.2f} tokens per word")`}</code></pre>
  <p class="note">
    Swap in any model you have pulled. If the file is bigger than <code>num_ctx</code>, the count tops out at that number, which is
    itself the lesson: the rest was cut off. See the <a href="/learn/activities/">cognitive activities</a> for what each kind of job costs.
  </p>

  <style>
    .budget{display:flex;height:34px;border-radius:8px;overflow:hidden;margin:12px 0;font-size:12px;color:#fff;font-weight:600}
    .budget span{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;animation:grow 2.4s ease-out both}
    .b1{background:#6b7280;flex:1}.b2{background:#2a78d6;flex:4;animation-delay:.3s!important}.b3{background:#a86f00;flex:2.2;animation-delay:.6s!important}.b4{background:#14855f;flex:1.4;animation-delay:.9s!important}
    @keyframes grow{from{flex-grow:.01;opacity:.2}}
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.budget span{animation:none}}
  </style>
</Base>
